I worked out the answers by hand using these facts:

- **Duplicate values can't be undone.** Once a value disappears from A, it can never return. So if A has all distinct values, any change to A makes the value set shrink, and B must contain only values still present.
- **Copies only spread along chains of steps of length at most K.** A value reaching a far position must pass through every position in between.
- **Small-N cases can be checked exhaustively.** With N=2, the first operation makes both entries equal, so only A=B or a result with both entries equal to a surviving value is possible.

